<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ming Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ming Dynasty, Ming dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ming Empire was the Chinese dynasty founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> in 1368 after the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>. It rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>, launched the treasure voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> and became a centre of global <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trade. It fell in 1644 to rebellion and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> conquest, which founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ming-empire.jpg" alt="Ming Empire" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Herrmann Creator:Q1509115 &amp; Georg Westermann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ming_Chinese_empire_1415_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the fifteenth century Brunei had become an independent Muslim sultanate with ties to <b>Ming China</b>, Malacca and the trading ports of the Sulu and Philippine seas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Diplomatic engagement, including tributary relations with <b>Ming China</b>, helped stabilise regional trade networks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…confederation of clans inhabiting the forested river valleys north-east of the Great Wall, whose unification under the chieftain Nurhaci between 1583 and 1616 transformed a fragmented tribal society into a cohesive military-political entity capable of challenging the faltering <b>Ming dynasty</b>, which had governed China for nearly three centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1372 King Satto of Chuzan answered an invitation from the Hongwu Emperor and sent tribute to <b>Ming China</b>, and soon all three principalities were trading with the Ming court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…he governed chiefly through terror, the speed of his armies and the appointment of sons and grandsons to regional commands, an arrangement that bred the succession struggles which began tearing the empire apart after his death at Otrar in February 1405, as he marched to invade <b>Ming China</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…this contest a single victor emerged: Zhu Yuanzhang, a former Buddhist monk of peasant origin, whose military and administrative ability enabled him to defeat his rivals Chen Youliang, at the great naval battle of Lake Poyang in 1363, and Zhang Shicheng, before proclaiming the <b>Ming Dynasty</b> in 1368.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
